# Namakkal Self-Respect Conference
Tamil title: நாமக்கல் சுயமரியாதை மாநாடு

This presidential address was delivered by Anna at the Self-Respect Conference held in Namakkal on 12 December 1937. The source notes that the address also bears the title “காந்திமதம்” (“Gandhism as a Religion”).

Comrades,

I offer my heartfelt thanks for the honour of presiding over your taluk conference. The victory drums of our successive conferences have created immense enthusiasm among comrades. In no movement is it easy to attain ideals rapidly. Our movement's ideals are such that, even after many years of struggle, further battlefields may continue to appear before us.

The character of a political party is to begin six months before an election, meet victory or defeat, dance in ecstasy if victorious, and yawn and go to sleep if defeated. Our movement, by contrast, maintains no direct connection with politics and takes social transformation as its objective. Whatever events may alter the condition of society, and for whatever reason they may occur, we are the people who must meet them. We possess an ideal held by no other movement.

We desire equality, fraternity and liberty to prevail in society. History tells us that these conditions have not existed in our country for many centuries. Securing equality, fraternity and liberty for the people is not something that can easily be accomplished. In European countries, revolutions took place and rivers of blood flowed merely to obtain the outlines of these ideals.

If equality is to prevail among the people, the doctrines obstructing it must be destroyed, and the movements that uphold those doctrines must be fought. The need for that struggle must be explained to the people, and they must be prepared to acquire the maturity and strength to participate in it. In brief, assembling that army is our first task. We have succeeded in it to some extent.

After a victory has been achieved, it may appear perfectly ordinary to us and we may even regard it casually. But in this country, where antiquity itself is regarded as sacred, who can deny that gathering a new army of knowledge is an incomparable victory?

## Before the Self-Respect Movement Appeared

Before our Self-Respect Movement arose, many movements, agitations and parties had existed in our country. Many died; some changed form; others became destitute and directionless.

Some agitations that arose here had a political tendency, while many others aimed at religious and social reform. Yet whether a movement belonged to politics or social reform, one common outlook could be seen among them: enthusiasm for antiquity.

The Besant movement, which played a major role in politics, preached the distinction of ancient India and the greatness of ancient life. There can be no doubt that “ancient greatness” meant Aryan greatness. If the summit of Aryan greatness was the doctrine of varnashrama, it is significant that the movement's woman leader was celebrated as an “aspect of the Goddess” and the “Mother of the World.” Religious ritual also dominated its programme of work. In the end its political activity subsided and Besant's movement became a motley religious movement.

Congress is moving toward the same condition. I shall explain that later.

The samajs and associations founded for social reform mostly placed religious devotion at their head. A religious covering enabled people to join those movements freely and without obstruction and to place unlimited faith in them. Yet those widely flourishing movements did not produce any tremendous transformation in society, because the light of knowledge did not enter in a manner that kindled popular enthusiasm for great change.

No movement immersed in religion can create transformation or a revolution of the mind. New philosophies, new gurus and commentaries may arise. Even when a programme somewhat contrary to a religion appeared, people had to take an ancient text and assign it a new meaning. Those movements did not begin the abolition of social disorders by openly explaining their causes.

## A First-Rate Revolution of the Mind

It was in this condition that our movement arose. Without fear, pity or deference, it emphasised how social disorders emerged and exposed how people were deceived in the name of religion; how foolish rituals and customs, supported by mythology, drained people's body, wealth and spirit; how the tyranny of varnashrama arose; how, because of these things, the Brahmin community of the Aryan class occupied the positions of brokers of religion, teachers of knowledge, social superiors and a power capable of making and unmaking governments; how abolishing Brahminism was the first step toward social equality; and how this antiquity had to be destroyed if society was to be renewed. This was the first revolution of the mind in Tamil Nadu.

## To Drive Out Brahminism

The harms created by Brahminism in society also appeared in politics. Only after seeing this plainly in Congress did the leader of our movement, E. V. R., establish the Self-Respect Movement.

In politics, the British enslaved us with bombs; in society, Brahmins enslaved others by pointing to the Vedas and declaring rules. People subjected to this latter form of slavery are the majority here. Comrade E. V. R. therefore showed that there was no benefit in such people launching a great liberation struggle to drive out the foreigner.

If we need the strength to drive away the foreign enemy, he said, there must be no one enslaving us here. If more people are to participate eagerly in expelling the British and obtaining self-government, the same passion for freedom must first arise within society.

Living in villages that include an agraharam, fearing cats and lizards before stepping outside, and holding the belief that marrying a widow violates God's command, how can we tell the British, “Who are you to rule our land? Leave this country”? Even if we say so, why would they leave?

For centuries we were enslaved to old texts. A social minority, without armed force and supposedly through “spiritual strength” alone, enslaved us for thousands of years. We cultivated the belief that another person was superior to us by birth—a *bhusura*, a god upon earth. There is therefore nothing surprising in our enslavement by the British artillery force. We must fight whatever fosters a mentality of servitude. It is for this reason that we criticised Congress.

Although we labour for freedom, we are not indifferent to national liberation. The leader of our movement and many of our supporters had passed through several fiery trials created by Congress. They realised that, however hard they and others worked, a defect in the doctrine would waste that labour without bringing the country any benefit.

## Rationalism Is Our Weapon

Therefore, using rationalism as our weapon, we began a difficult struggle against Indian religious imperialism. Our opponents attacked us using mythologies and ancient customs as weapons.

We said that no superiority or inferiority should exist between people in social life. They replied that hierarchy had prevailed in this country for ages with the authority of religious scripture, mythology and epic, and had been accepted and protected by emperors.

We said social life should be conducted with knowledge, inquiry and experience as its supports. They said that following Manu was a religious command. We exposed the obscene mythologies that cultivate servility and the cruelty of scriptures that foster communal autocracy.

Through our intense engagement in this work, the movement became firmly established in people's minds, especially those of the young. The world of youth began to welcome even the most radical doctrine. We taught social equality, and young people rejoiced. In this condition, changes occurred in Tamil Nadu's political world.

## How Congress Became a New Religion

Congress, which said it would liberate India from the British, slowly became a religion at the same time. Bharat Mata was created, and a Mahatma arose. He began to appear holding one thing in one hand and a spinning wheel in the other. Fasting, worship and the inner voice began to shine and emit their fragrance in his political life. “National” rituals modelled upon religious ceremonies were initiated.

Religion had fostered an outlook in which, however many cruelties occurred in its name and however selfish people used it, exposing those wrongs was considered sinful and the critic branded a traitor to religion. Gandhism grew in the same manner. Lovers of antiquity became enchanted with it. Even people who understood the cruelty of varnashrama began, because Gandhi said so, openly to support varnashrama dharma and declare that they laboured to revive an ancient system of government founded upon it.

This became a rare opportunity for Brahmins, who had long served as brokers of religion and maintained class arrogance. Just as Brahminism's grip was loosening, it obtained Gandhism as armour. This greatly benefited Brahminical religion.

Brahminism's proposition that whatever a person called “great” says must be followed merely because he says it—even when contrary to knowledge and history—became an important feature of politics. For ages, Brahminism had taught people to believe that religion alone would elevate human life and, even when deceived, never to doubt that belief. Congress teaching was delivered in the same manner.

As soon as people began to think that Congress methods such as non-cooperation, civil disobedience and the Salt Satyagraha had failed, they were told: “The Mahatma remains; Congress remains; trust it, and our country will obtain deliverance.” What is this other than the propagation of a new religion?

## Congress and the Other Political Parties

Congress's stage-managers had reason to give it the epithet “divine assembly.” Other parties contain orthodox believers and religious zealots too. There are Justice Party ministers passionately devoted to feasting their eyes upon the milk-anointing of Palaniappan. The Justice Party also contains lovers of antiquity who dislike the intensity of the Self-Respect Movement. We supported them in the last election. Why? Our enemies habitually distort the reason.

There are religious zealots and orthodox believers among the Justice Party's leaders. But in that party, political work was not transformed into religion. It was an ordinary political party without Bharat Mata, a Mahatma or prayer beads. Supporting it therefore cannot be called support for orthodoxy. It performed only political work and was not made into a religion that cultivated servility. Moreover, it destroyed the political domination of Brahmins, the guardians of the fortress of orthodoxy. To that extent, it became worthy of our affection.

## The Present Condition

If we now enter politics in order to continue our social-reform work, how can we refrain from condemning a priestly regime in which a new religion is preached and its priests squeeze their “devotees” dry and live upon offerings?

In keeping with the conduct of the Madras Ministry, its leader bears the hereditary title “Acharya.” I hear that in your district he is called the “deity beneath the peepal tree.” According to religious command, it is the duty of the supreme devotee to carry out upon his head every command issued by the Acharya. In the same manner, Congress legislators, the press and Congress supporters spend their days chanting, “We take refuge at Acharya's sacred feet.”

Is this not Congress rule? Should it not be engaged in driving out the British, resisting imperialism and attacking new bonds of servitude? Instead, the Acharya Ministry dines with the Governor and Collectors, makes plans with them and praises the official class. If this is questioned, the immediate reply is, “Acharya knows everything; trust Congress.” What is this other than the style of a religion?

We must not forget that this Ministry was formed with the promise of doing immense good and possesses an “unshakeable” majority. Under such an Assembly, expenditure has not decreased even half a coin beyond what the “reactionaries” reduced. To conduct the Government without borrowing as the “reactionaries” did, this “eldest son of the divine assembly” began by borrowing one and a half crore rupees.

The British have not left and British salaries have not fallen. Ministers' salaries alone were reduced; neither ministerial expenditure nor the Government's enormous expenditure has declined. It has now been plainly stated that prohibition will not be introduced throughout all districts under this warmly welcomed rule.

This administration frequently suffers a “chill” at the thought of what to do if the Governor intervenes. Calling the national flag the Congress flag, appointing a British person to the medical college and defeating the moratorium bill all arose from that chill.

Under this rule, not one repressive measure has been omitted: punishment of worker strikes under Sections 144 and 124A, pressure upon socialists, lathi charges, securities demanded from newspapers and house searches. We see the obstinacy, autocracy and Hitlerism of the declaration, “Even if Tamils oppose it, I do not care; I shall introduce Hindi.” Under this rule, traitors receive compassion and workers receive punishment. The beauty of its administration has been revealed in the chapter of corruption in the Madras Corporation.

If anyone who comes forward to criticise and condemn an administration bearing so many deformities is abused and treated with suspicion, what does that reveal? It shows that Congress is being turned into a new religion.

Just as religion created disorders in society, this new religion will create only harm in politics and, through politics, in society. For this important reason, our movement must henceforth expose the propagation of this religion and the evils it produces. Just as we suppressed frauds in religion, we must suppress frauds in politics. Today the two appear together.

I therefore ask the conference to examine and make the necessary arrangements to strengthen the means of exposing that fraud and to carry our propaganda into the villages.

Once again, I offer my thanks to you for granting me this honour.

---NOTES---
The source explicitly gives this presidential address the alternate title “காந்திமதம்,” rendered contextually as “Gandhism as a Religion.”

This is a 1937 political speech. Its historical terminology and criticisms of communities, religions and named political movements are translated as Anna used them; they are not presented as contemporary editorial judgments.

The OCR phrase following “பாரத மாதா சிருஷ்டிக்கப்பட்டாள்; மகாத்மா ஏற்பட்டார்” reads “ஒரு கரத்தில் இதையும்,” but the referent of “இதையும்” appears lost or damaged. The translation therefore leaves it nonspecific rather than inventing an object.

The source's closing editorial note appears to request missing Anna speeches from a span of pages or years, but its numerals are severely OCR-damaged and are retained only in the Tamil source.
